## 2. Unsupervised Models

| Model | Concept | Pros | Cons | How It Helps the Detection Idea |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Autoencoder (AE/VAE)** | Compresses input data into a lower-dimensional bottleneck and reconstructs it back. | Excellent for Zero-day attack detection; trains only on normal traffic without needing any labels. | High sensitivity in calibrating the reconstruction-error threshold, risking high false alarms. | **Zero-Day Detection:** Catches completely unknown, zero-day attacks by flagging high reconstruction errors. |
| **GAN (Generative Adversarial Network)** | Two neural networks (Generator & Discriminator) compete against each other to create realistic data. | Generates highly realistic synthetic traffic to fix data imbalance and tests the system's defensive limits. | Highly unstable training (Mode Collapse); risk of being exploited by attackers to bypass security. | **Dataset Balancing:** Generates realistic synthetic attack traffic to ensure the IDS trains well on rare threats. |
| **GNN (Graph Neural Network)** | Represents network traffic as a graph structured of connected devices (nodes) and flows (edges). | Successfully captures topological attacks, Botnet fan-out, and lateral movements across the network. | Heavy computation and memory overhead on the SDN Controller due to real-time graph updating. | **Relational Anomalies:** Spots infected devices spreading malware or communicating inside a Botnet. |
| **Diffusion Model** | Generates data by systematically adding noise to data and learning to reverse the process to remove it. | Generates extremely high-fidelity and diverse synthetic attack data with more stable training than GANs. | Iterative sampling process is very slow, making it impossible to use for real-time traffic detection. | **Offline Data Augmentation:** Provides varied training data offline, preparing the detector for complex mutations. |

